		{
			"id": "112858081725791673",
			"parentId": "112854602955453396",
			"url": "https://mstdn.social/@brawaru/112858081486705979",
			"author": {
				"name": "Braw ☕🏳️‍🌈",
				"url": "https://mstdn.social/@brawaru",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/380/201/982/554/664/original/4cd79a5fec5ff5fb.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-07-27T10:54:19.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> oh I see the the error locally, exactly what I suspected: TypeScript cannot really do magic of analysing the code flow deeply so it sees that `this.plugins` is not initialised in the constructor, but that you also define `this.plugins` in other place, so it consider it potentially `undefined`. You can simply initialise `plugins` at the top of the class to tell TS ‘trust me bro, it's there and it's never `undefined`’ (even though if forget to call `reset` in constructor, it will be 💀)</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
